import{_ as a,C as n,o as r,c as i,j as t,a as o,E as d,a7 as c}from"./chunks/framework.C4ZIv4ii.js";const b=JSON.parse('{"title":"Falcon Security","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/security.md","filePath":"guide/security.md","lastUpdated":1792587806000}'),l={name:"guide/security.md"},h={id:"falcon-security",tabindex:"-1"};function u(g,e,p,f,y,m){const s=n("Badge");return r(),i("div",null,[t("h1",h,[e[0]||(e[0]=o("Falcon Security ",-1)),d(s,{type:"warning",text:"Pro"}),e[1]||(e[1]=o()),e[2]||(e[2]=t("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#falcon-security","aria-label":'Permalink to "Falcon Security <Badge type="warning" text="Pro" />"'},"​",-1))]),e[3]||(e[3]=c('<p>The <strong>Falcon Security</strong> plugin adds two-factor sign-in, sign-in alerts, a password policy, a firewall, a malware scanner and a security audit. It ships with <a href="/falconcms/guide/pro.html">FalconCMS Pro</a>. Activate it under <strong>Plugins</strong>, then open <strong>Security</strong> in the admin menu.</p><h2 id="two-factor-sign-in" tabindex="-1">Two-factor sign-in <a class="header-anchor" href="#two-factor-sign-in" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Two-factor sign-in&quot;">​</a></h2><p>After the password, the site asks for a 6-digit code from an authenticator app on your phone, so a stolen password is not enough to get in. Any app works: Google Authenticator, Microsoft Authenticator, Authy, 1Password, Bitwarden.</p><p><strong>Setting it up</strong> — open <strong>Security → My security</strong> (every user can, whatever their role), scan the QR code with the app, type the code it shows and press <strong>Turn on</strong>. Save the 8 recovery codes it then shows: if you lose your phone, each one signs you in once.</p><p><strong>Signing in</strong> — after the password, the sign-in page asks for the code from the app, below your email (a pasted 6-digit code is sent at once). <strong>Not you?</strong> goes back to the password. Tick <strong>Don&#39;t ask again on this device</strong> to skip the code on that browser for 30 days (change the number in <strong>Security → Sign-in</strong>; 0 always asks).</p><p><strong>Wrong codes lock the account</strong> — whoever reaches the code step already knew the password. After 5 wrong codes (adjustable) the account is locked for 30 minutes: the sign-in page refuses it even with the right password, and the account&#39;s owner gets an email telling them someone knows their password. Starting over does not reset the count. An administrator can lift the lock early under <strong>Security → Firewall → Unblock</strong>.</p><p><strong>Requiring it</strong> — in <strong>Security → Sign-in</strong>, tick the roles that must use it. Every role is listed, including ones plugins add (Wholesale Customer, say), and a user&#39;s extra roles count too. The users list there shows everyone, with a role filter and a search. Someone in such a role who has not set it up is sent to the setup screen the next time they open the admin. Set it up for yourself first.</p><p><strong>Lost phone</strong> — sign in with a recovery code, or ask an administrator to <strong>Reset</strong> your two-factor in <strong>Security → Sign-in</strong>; you then sign in with your password and set it up again.</p><h2 id="shop-checkout-card-testing" tabindex="-1">Shop checkout: card testing <a class="header-anchor" href="#shop-checkout-card-testing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Shop checkout: card testing&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Thieves test stolen cards with many small orders; each decline can cost a fee or a chargeback, and payment providers close shops where it goes on. <strong>Security → Firewall → Shop checkout</strong> watches the declines: after 3 declined payments from one address in an hour (or 5 for one email), that address may not place orders for 24 hours; more than 5 orders in 10 minutes from one address are refused; and if many payments are declined across the shop, the administrators are emailed and the limits tighten. Paused addresses are listed with <strong>Let through</strong>. Browsing is never affected.</p><h2 id="admin-only-from-your-addresses" tabindex="-1">Admin only from your addresses <a class="header-anchor" href="#admin-only-from-your-addresses" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Admin only from your addresses&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Security → Firewall → Admin only from these addresses</strong>: list your office and home addresses, and anywhere else the admin and its sign-in page simply do not exist (a &quot;not found&quot; page). The public site and the shop are not affected. Your current address must be in the list, so you cannot lock yourself out by saving it. If your address changes, add <code>FALCON_FIREWALL=off</code> to <code>.env</code> or run <code>php artisan falcon:security-allowlist off</code> (or <code>--add=</code> your new address).</p><h2 id="fake-search-engine-bots" tabindex="-1">Fake search-engine bots <a class="header-anchor" href="#fake-search-engine-bots" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Fake search-engine bots&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Many bots call themselves Googlebot or Bingbot to get past protections. With <strong>Block fake search-engine bots</strong> on, such a visitor is checked against the search engine&#39;s own addresses (the way Google and Bing ask sites to check); real crawlers pass, impostors are refused, and those that keep trying are banned.</p><h2 id="live-traffic" tabindex="-1">Live traffic <a class="header-anchor" href="#live-traffic" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Live traffic&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Security → Live</strong> shows requests as they happen: address and country, what was asked for, who (a signed-in user, a browser or a bot) and what happened — including why the firewall refused it. <strong>Block</strong> and <strong>Unblock</strong> sit beside every row. Recording writes a line per visit, so you start it for an hour, 4 or 24 and it stops by itself; the lines are kept a day.</p><h2 id="passkeys" tabindex="-1">Passkeys <a class="header-anchor" href="#passkeys" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Passkeys&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A passkey signs you in with your phone&#39;s fingerprint or face, Windows Hello or a security key — no password and no code. It only works on your site, so a fake login page cannot steal it.</p><p><strong>Adding one</strong> — <strong>Security → My security → Passkeys</strong>, give it a name and press <strong>Add a passkey</strong>; your browser or phone does the rest. Passkeys need HTTPS (or <code>localhost</code>).</p><p><strong>Signing in</strong> — the login page shows <strong>Sign in with a passkey</strong> once any account has one. It counts as two-factor, so no code is asked for after it; the two-factor step also offers <strong>Use a passkey instead</strong>.</p><h2 id="temporary-access" tabindex="-1">Temporary access <a class="header-anchor" href="#temporary-access" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Temporary access&quot;">​</a></h2><p>For a developer or a helper: <strong>Security → Access</strong>, enter their name, email, a role and how long (1 to 90 days), and send the invitation. They choose their own password — and set up two-factor on their own phone, if you ask for it — so nobody shares a password or a phone. When the time is up the account is blocked and signed out of every browser; its history stays in the logs. You can <strong>Extend</strong> it, <strong>End now</strong>, or <strong>Send again</strong> if the invitation did not arrive.</p><h2 id="lockdown" tabindex="-1">Lockdown <a class="header-anchor" href="#lockdown" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Lockdown&quot;">​</a></h2><p>When an account may be in the wrong hands: <strong>Security → Lockdown</strong>. Tick who keeps full access (you always do), choose how long, and confirm with your password. For everyone else the admin and the API turn read-only — nothing can be saved, deleted or installed — and you can sign them out and keep them out until it ends. The public site and the shop keep working. Only the people you named can end it early; from the server, <code>php artisan falcon:security-lockdown off</code>.</p><h2 id="security-log" tabindex="-1">Security log <a class="header-anchor" href="#security-log" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Security log&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Security → Log</strong> keeps sign-ins (and failed ones), password and role changes, accounts created or deleted, two-factor and passkey changes, plugins and themes installed, settings changes, bans and malware removed — for 90 days by default, separately from the activity log. Filter by type, severity, user and date, and export to CSV. The administrators get an email for risky events: many deletions by one user in a short time, the activity log cleared, a plugin or theme installed, an account guessed at from several addresses, a lockdown.</p><h2 id="sign-in-alerts-and-sessions" tabindex="-1">Sign-in alerts and sessions <a class="header-anchor" href="#sign-in-alerts-and-sessions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Sign-in alerts and sessions&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>New sign-in</strong> — when an account signs in from a network it has never used, its owner gets a &quot;was this you?&quot; email with the address, country and browser. Choose the roles it covers, and an address that gets a copy, in <strong>Security → Sign-in</strong>. The first sign-in after the plugin is switched on is only remembered.</p><p><strong>New administrator</strong> — the other administrators get an email when an account is made an administrator.</p><p><strong>Log out everywhere</strong> — <strong>Security → My security</strong> lists the browsers you are signed in on (when sessions are kept in the database) and signs you out of every other one. An administrator can do it for any user from <strong>Security → Sign-in</strong> — use it when an account may be in the wrong hands. Remember-me cookies and trusted browsers stop working too.</p><h2 id="password-policy" tabindex="-1">Password policy <a class="header-anchor" href="#password-policy" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Password policy&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every form that sets a password — registration, password resets, users added or edited, the shop&#39;s account page — must meet the policy set in <strong>Security → Sign-in</strong>: a minimum length (10 by default), letters and numbers, optionally a symbol, and not a password known from a data breach. The breach check uses <a href="https://haveibeenpwned.com/Passwords" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Have I Been Pwned</a> without sending the password: only the first 5 characters of its hash leave the site. If the service cannot be reached, the password is allowed.</p><h2 id="firewall" tabindex="-1">Firewall <a class="header-anchor" href="#firewall" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Firewall&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The firewall checks every request before the site handles it, and refuses:</p><ul><li>addresses and ranges you block (<code>203.0.113.5</code>, <code>203.0.113.0/24</code>, <code>2001:db8::/32</code>);</li><li>visitors from countries you block (known behind Cloudflare, or for visitors the site has looked up before);</li><li><strong>probes</strong> — requests for <code>/.env</code>, <code>/.git</code>, <code>/wp-login.php</code>, <code>/phpmyadmin</code>, backup archives and the like, which only attackers ask for on a FalconCMS site. Three probes in ten minutes ban the address;</li><li><strong>floods</strong> — more than 40 &quot;not found&quot; pages, or 10 sign-in attempts (the dashboard login, the shop&#39;s My Account sign-in, the wholesale login, passkeys and magic-link requests), a minute from one address earn a ban;</li><li><strong>attacks</strong> — SQL injection, cross-site scripting, path traversal, file inclusion, command injection and Log4Shell payloads in the address or a form (the admin area is not inspected). Repeat offenders are banned like probes; switch it off in <strong>Security → Firewall</strong>;</li><li>optionally, requests naming another site in their Host header.</li></ul><p>Bans last 24 hours by default. Everything is adjustable in <strong>Security → Firewall</strong>, which also lists the bans, what the firewall stopped, and the sign-in form&#39;s own lockouts (with <strong>Unblock</strong>).</p><p><strong>Safety valves</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Log only</strong> records what would be blocked and blocks nothing — use it to try rules out.</li><li>An administrator&#39;s address is never blocked once they have signed in from it.</li><li>Locked out anyway? Add <code>FALCON_FIREWALL=off</code> to the site&#39;s <code>.env</code>.</li><li>Behind Cloudflare, the site must trust Cloudflare&#39;s addresses (TrustProxies). Until it does, every visitor seems to come from Cloudflare, so the firewall only records, and the audit tells you how to fix it.</li></ul><h2 id="malware-scanner" tabindex="-1">Malware scanner <a class="header-anchor" href="#malware-scanner" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Malware scanner&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Security → Scanner → Run the first scan</strong> checks the site&#39;s files and database for:</p><ul><li>PHP files in the uploads folder (or PHP hidden inside an &quot;image&quot;), and unexpected PHP in <code>public/</code>;</li><li>code that runs hidden (decoded) instructions, passes visitor input to the shell, or matches a known web shell;</li><li>server files rewritten to redirect visitors or run a file before every request;</li><li>scripts, miners, invisible iframes and hidden links injected into pages or the Customizer;</li><li>code <strong>changed or added since the baseline</strong> — the first scan records the site as it is as the clean state, so run it while you trust the site. After an update, press <strong>Accept the current files as the baseline</strong>.</li></ul><p>It looks through the site&#39;s own code — <code>app/</code>, <code>bootstrap/</code>, <code>config/</code>, <code>routes/</code>, <code>database/</code>, <code>lang/</code>, the views, themes and plugins, the CMS itself — the files at the top of the project, the uploads and <code>public/</code>. Third-party packages in the rest of <code>vendor/</code> are left to Composer.</p><p>Tick findings (or <strong>Select all</strong> in a tab) for a <strong>bulk action</strong>: remove (each finding gets its recommended, low-risk removal; risky ones are skipped for you to decide one by one), quarantine, mark safe, restore or undo. All findings about one file move together.</p><p>For each finding:</p><ul><li><strong>Remove the code</strong> — when malicious code was injected into a file or post the site needs, only that code is cut out; the rest stays. You see exactly what will go before you confirm. If it cannot be cut out without breaking the file, the option is not offered.</li><li><strong>Delete file</strong> — for a file that should not exist at all (PHP in the uploads, a web shell, a file that is nothing but malicious code). Deleting a file the site had when it was clean is marked <strong>high risk</strong>: you must type its name to confirm. Files the site cannot start without (<code>public/index.php</code>, <code>bootstrap/app.php</code>) are never deleted.</li><li><strong>Quarantine</strong> moves a file out of reach (restore it from the Quarantined tab), <strong>It&#39;s safe</strong> hides it until the file changes.</li></ul><p>Before anything is removed, a dialog explains what goes and that data may be lost, and asks you to confirm. A backup is kept for 30 days: <strong>Removed → Undo</strong> puts it back. The scanner never removes anything on its own. It also runs every night (with the site&#39;s scheduler) and emails new findings; both can be turned off on the screen. From the command line: <code>php artisan falcon:security-scan</code> (<code>--baseline</code> to accept the current files).</p><h2 id="security-audit" tabindex="-1">Security audit <a class="header-anchor" href="#security-audit" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Security audit&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Security → Audit → Run the audit</strong> checks the site for the setup mistakes attackers try first, and says how to fix each one: debug mode left on, the <code>.env</code> file, <code>.git</code> folder or logs reachable from the web, no HTTPS, an insecure session cookie, a wrong APP_URL, Cloudflare not trusted, an outdated FalconCMS or PHP, administrators without two-factor, a default <code>admin@admin.com</code> account, the default sign-in address, the firewall switched off, no bot check on the sign-in form, storage folders the site cannot write to, no password policy, and malware findings or a scan long overdue.</p><p><strong>Security → Overview</strong> shows the firewall, two-factor use among administrators, the malware scanner, the audit score and the latest firewall events at a glance.</p>',49))])}const k=a(l,[["render",u]]);export{b as __pageData,k as default};
